<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacobin Club</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a>–<a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Jacobins</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jacobin Club was the most influential political club of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, named after the former Dominican convent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> where it met, the Dominicans being known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as Jacobins. Under Robespierre it became the centre of radical republicanism and backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reign-of-terror">Reign of Terror</a> of 1793–94, and it was closed after his fall. The name Jacobins was later used across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for radical republicans and supporters of the French revolutionary cause.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacobin-club.jpg" alt="Jacobin Club" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author Vector redesign by VectorVoyager · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Jacobins_of_Paris_(Republican).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The club began in 1789 as the Club Breton, a group of Breton deputies at the Estates General at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>, and deputies from other regions soon joined. Early members included the comte de Mirabeau, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Abbé Sieyès</a>, Antoine Barnave and Robespierre. It was re-founded in November 1789 and rented the refectory of the Dominican monastery of the Jacobins in the Rue Saint-Honoré, next to the Assembly. In January 1790 it took the name Société des amis de la Constitution, though its opponents had already dubbed it the Jacobins.</p><p>Once in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> the club admitted citizens besides deputies. There were one hundred and fifty-two affiliated clubs by August 1790 and, at the peak, at least 7,000 chapters with a membership estimated at half a million or more. Women were not members but could follow debates from the balconies, and the high subscription confined membership to well-off men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The club supported the monarchy until the eve of the Republic on 20 September 1792. In late 1791 a group around Brissot, later called Girondins, urged war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, which Robespierre opposed in the club; the war was decided in April 1792, and the two groups then polarised. On 2 June 1793 a crowd of armed men besieged the Convention, which expelled 22 leading Girondins. Robespierre and the Mountain then dominated the government until July 1794. In October 1793 twenty-one Girondins were sentenced to death, and between September 1793 and July 1794 there were 17,000 death sentences across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>On 28 July 1794 the Convention had Robespierre and 21 associates guillotined. The club's membership fell to about 600, and gangs of the jeunesse dorée attacked it. On 12 November 1794 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> decreed its permanent closure by a nearly unanimous vote, and within a year 93 percent of the Jacobin clubs had closed. An attempt to revive it in July 1799, the Club du Manège, was suppressed after about a month.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In power the Jacobins completed the abolition of feudalism, decided in principle on 4 August 1789 but held back by a clause requiring compensation. They set up the Cult of Reason and the Cult of the Supreme Being in place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. They consolidated republicanism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, while their unjudicial methods discredited the Revolution in many eyes. The term Jacobin was later applied to radicals of similar views.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacobins” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Moreno and his friends, inspired by the French Revolution and called <b>Jacobins</b> by their opponents, drove its policies; Saavedra's followers were more moderate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Others have suggested that the song mocked the Dominican friars, called <b>Jacobins</b> in France, for idleness and comfort.</blockquote><a href="/songs/frere-jacques">Frère Jacques</a></li><li><blockquote>The playwright Olympe de Gouges, author of the Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen, was executed during the Terror for writings attacking the <b>Jacobins</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-3">3 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic was governed by a provisional administration of local <b>Jacobins</b> under French military control.</blockquote><a href="/state/anconine-republic">Anconine Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The Commune's council, a mix of <b>Jacobins</b>, socialists and followers of Proudhon and Blanqui, separated church and state, abolished night work in bakeries, let workers take over abandoned workshops and postponed rents.</blockquote><a href="/state/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a></li><li><blockquote>As Napoleon's army drove back the Piedmontese troops of the Kingdom of Sardinia in April 1796, local <b>Jacobins</b> led by Ignazio Bonafous and the exiled publicist Giovanni Antonio Ranza entered Alba and proclaimed a republic, hoping for French support for a revolution in Piedmont.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-alba">Republic of Alba</a></li></ul></section>
